            <ArticleTitle>Pseudotyped baculovirus is an effective gene expression tool for studying molecular function during axolotl limb regeneration.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>262-275</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0012-1606(17)30438-4</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.ydbio.2017.10.008</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Axolotls can regenerate complex structures through recruitment and remodeling of cells within mature tissues. Accessing the underlying mechanisms at a molecular resolution is crucial to understand how injury triggers regeneration and how it proceeds. However, gene transformation in adult tissues can be challenging. Here we characterize the use of pseudotyped baculovirus (BV) as an effective gene transfer method both for cells within mature limb tissue and within the blastema. These cells remain competent to participate in regeneration after transduction. We further characterize the effectiveness of BV for gene overexpression studies by overexpressing Shh in the blastema, which yields a high penetrance of classic polydactyly phenotypes. Overall, our work establishes BV as a powerful tool to access gene function in axolotl limb regeneration.</AbstractText>
